# Accepted vulnerability findings: the one escape hatch for docker.yml's
# release scan, which otherwise blocks `publish` on any fixable HIGH or
# CRITICAL finding in either image. image-scan.yml's weekly scan of the
# published images reads this file too, so an accepted finding does not
# nag there either.
#
# Add an entry only when a rebuild cannot clear the finding yet (the fix is
# not in the pinned base image, or it does not apply to how the image uses
# the package), never to get a release out past a fix that exists. Every
# entry carries, on the comment lines directly above it:
#
#   - why the finding does not apply, or why it is accepted until the expiry
#   - who accepted it, and when
#
# and an `exp:` date on the entry itself, after which trivy stops ignoring
# it and the scan blocks again. Keep expiries short (weeks, not months); an
# expired entry is a prompt to look again, not to extend it by default.
#
# Format (one vulnerability id per line, trivy's plain .trivyignore format):
#
#   # CVE-2026-00000: only reachable through <feature>, which neither image
#   # builds. Accepted by <name>, 2026-01-01.
#   CVE-2026-00000 exp:2026-02-01
#
# No entries.
